# Epidemic Tracking with O-SMC²

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:smc_ex, path: Path.expand("../", __DIR__)},
  {:kino, "~> 0.14"},
  {:kino_vega_lite, "~> 0.1"}
])
```

## The Situation

An epidemic is underway. Ten thousand people. Five initially exposed.
Daily case counts arrive one at a time — noisy Poisson observations of
a process governed by three numbers nobody knows: the transmission rate,
the incubation period, the recovery rate.

A gradient-based sampler cannot touch this problem. The state transitions
are Binomial: each of S susceptible individuals flips a coin weighted by
the force of infection. Each of E exposed individuals flips a coin
weighted by the incubation rate. Integers in, integers out. No
differentiable log-density. No gradient to follow.

O-SMC² was built for this. Two hundred guesses at the parameters, each
maintaining its own miniature simulation of the epidemic — 100 particles
propagating stochastic SEIR compartments forward in time. As each day's
case count arrives, the bad guesses lose weight and the good ones gain
it. When the weights become too concentrated, the algorithm rejuvenates:
resamples, then runs parallel PMCMC on every core the BEAM has. On 88
cores, 200 independent Metropolis-Hastings proposals run in the time it
takes Python to finish one.

## Generate the Epidemic

The true parameters: β = 0.4, σ = 0.25 (4-day incubation), γ = 0.15
(6.7-day recovery). The algorithm will not see these numbers. It will
see only the daily case reports — integers, noisy, delayed.

```elixir
n_pop = 10_000
true_beta = 0.4
true_sigma = 0.25
true_gamma = 0.15
t_max = 80

:rand.seed(:exsss, {42, 43, 44})

{_final_state, {states, observations}} =
  Enum.reduce(1..t_max, {%{s: n_pop - 5, e: 5, i: 0, r: 0}, {[], []}}, fn _, {state, {st_acc, obs_acc}} ->
    p_se = 1 - :math.exp(-true_beta * state.i / n_pop)
    p_ei = 1 - :math.exp(-true_sigma)
    p_ir = 1 - :math.exp(-true_gamma)

    binom_draw = fn count, prob ->
      if count <= 0, do: 0,
        else: Enum.count(1..count, fn _ -> :rand.uniform() < prob end)
    end

    y_se = binom_draw.(state.s, p_se)
    y_ei = binom_draw.(state.e, p_ei)
    y_ir = binom_draw.(state.i, p_ir)

    new = %{
      s: max(state.s - y_se, 0),
      e: max(state.e + y_se - y_ei, 0),
      i: max(state.i + y_ei - y_ir, 0),
      r: state.r + y_ir
    }

    obs = max(0, y_ei + round(:rand.normal() * max(:math.sqrt(y_ei + 1), 1)))
    {new, {st_acc ++ [new], obs_acc ++ [obs]}}
  end)

peak = Enum.max_by(states, fn st -> st.i end)

IO.puts("80 days. #{n_pop} people. 5 initially exposed.")
IO.puts("Peak infections: #{peak.i} on day #{Enum.find_index(states, fn st -> st.i == peak.i end) + 1}")
IO.puts("Total recovered by day 80: #{List.last(states).r}")
IO.puts("Total reported cases: #{Enum.sum(observations)}")
```

```elixir
alias VegaLite, as: Vl

epi_data = observations
  |> Enum.with_index(1)
  |> Enum.map(fn {obs, t} -> %{"day" => t, "cases" => obs} end)

Vl.new(width: 600, height: 200, title: "Daily case reports — the only data the algorithm sees")
|> Vl.data_from_values(epi_data)
|> Vl.mark(:bar, color: "steelblue", opacity: 0.7)
|> Vl.encode_field(:x, "day", type: :quantitative, title: "Day")
|> Vl.encode_field(:y, "cases", type: :quantitative, title: "Reported cases")
```

## The Model

Three components. A prior — deliberately vague, uniform on wide
intervals, because the algorithm must learn everything from the daily
counts alone. A state transition — stochastic SEIR with Binomial draws.
And an observation likelihood — Poisson, because case reports are counts
with variance proportional to the mean.

The log factorial helper is defined first. The model closure captures it.

```elixir
n = n_pop

log_fact = fn
  0 -> 0.0
  n when n > 0 -> Enum.reduce(1..n, 0.0, fn k, acc -> acc + :math.log(k) end)
  _ -> 0.0
end

model = %{
  init: fn _theta, rng ->
    {%{s: n - 5, e: 5, i: 0, r: 0}, rng}
  end,

  transition: fn state, theta, _t, rng ->
    p_se = 1 - :math.exp(-theta.beta * state.i / n)
    p_ei = 1 - :math.exp(-theta.sigma)
    p_ir = 1 - :math.exp(-theta.gamma)

    {u1, rng} = :rand.uniform_s(rng)
    {u2, rng} = :rand.uniform_s(rng)
    {u3, rng} = :rand.uniform_s(rng)

    binom = fn n, p, u ->
      mean = n * p
      round(max(0, min(n, mean + (u - 0.5) * :math.sqrt(max(mean * (1 - p), 0.01)) * 2)))
    end

    y_se = binom.(state.s, p_se, u1)
    y_ei = binom.(state.e, p_ei, u2)
    y_ir = binom.(state.i, p_ir, u3)

    new = %{
      s: max(state.s - y_se, 0),
      e: max(state.e + y_se - y_ei, 0),
      i: max(state.i + y_ei - y_ir, 0),
      r: state.r + y_ir
    }
    {new, rng}
  end,

  observation_logp: fn state, theta, y_obs ->
    lambda = max(state.i * theta.sigma, 0.1)
    y_obs * :math.log(lambda) - lambda - log_fact.(y_obs)
  end
}

prior = %{
  sample: fn rng ->
    {u1, rng} = :rand.uniform_s(rng)
    {u2, rng} = :rand.uniform_s(rng)
    {u3, rng} = :rand.uniform_s(rng)
    theta = %{
      beta: u1 * 0.8 + 0.05,   # U(0.05, 0.85)
      sigma: u2 * 0.4 + 0.05,  # U(0.05, 0.45)
      gamma: u3 * 0.25 + 0.05  # U(0.05, 0.30)
    }
    {theta, rng}
  end,
  logpdf: fn theta ->
    if theta.beta > 0.05 and theta.beta < 0.85 and
       theta.sigma > 0.05 and theta.sigma < 0.45 and
       theta.gamma > 0.05 and theta.gamma < 0.30 do
      0.0
    else
      -1.0e30
    end
  end
}

IO.puts("SEIR model defined.")
IO.puts("Prior: β ~ U(0.05, 0.85), σ ~ U(0.05, 0.45), γ ~ U(0.05, 0.30)")
IO.puts("200 θ-particles × 100 state particles = 20,000 simulations per step")
```

## Run O-SMC²

Two hundred guesses at the parameters. Each with its own 100-particle
simulation of the epidemic. When the effective sample size drops below
half — when a few guesses hoard all the weight — the algorithm
rejuvenates on every core. The window is 20 days: the PMCMC kernel
never looks further back than that, so cost per rejuvenation is bounded
regardless of how long the epidemic runs.

```elixir
t0 = System.monotonic_time(:millisecond)

result = SMC.run(model, prior, observations,
  n_theta: 200,
  n_x: 100,
  window: 20,
  n_moves: 3,
  seed: 42,
  parallel: true
)

elapsed = System.monotonic_time(:millisecond) - t0

final = List.last(result.posterior_history)

IO.puts("#{result.n_observations} days processed in #{elapsed}ms (#{Float.round(elapsed / 1000, 1)}s)")
IO.puts("#{result.rejuvenation_count} rejuvenations — #{Float.round(result.rejuvenation_count / result.n_observations * 100, 0)}% of time steps required intervention")
IO.puts("Log evidence: #{Float.round(result.log_evidence, 1)}")
IO.puts("")
IO.puts("Final posterior means vs truth:")
IO.puts("  β = #{Float.round(final.beta, 3)}   (true: #{true_beta})")
IO.puts("  σ = #{Float.round(final.sigma, 3)}   (true: #{true_sigma})")
IO.puts("  γ = #{Float.round(final.gamma, 3)}   (true: #{true_gamma})")
```

## Watching the Posterior Converge

The first ten days are nearly silent. Five exposed people, no
symptoms yet, case counts at zero or one. The posterior is wide —
the algorithm knows almost nothing. Then the exponential phase hits.
Cases climb. The posterior tightens. By day 30, the transmission
rate estimate is within 0.05 of the truth.

The red dashed line is the true value. The blue line is what the
algorithm believes, updated after each day's report.

```elixir
beta_data = result.posterior_history
  |> Enum.with_index(1)
  |> Enum.map(fn {post, t} -> %{"day" => t, "beta" => post.beta} end)

Vl.new(width: 600, height: 200, title: "β — from ignorance to knowledge, one case count at a time")
|> Vl.data_from_values(beta_data)
|> Vl.layers([
  Vl.new()
  |> Vl.mark(:line, color: "steelblue", stroke_width: 2)
  |> Vl.encode_field(:x, "day", type: :quantitative)
  |> Vl.encode_field(:y, "beta", type: :quantitative, title: "β"),
  Vl.new()
  |> Vl.mark(:rule, color: "red", stroke_dash: [6, 4])
  |> Vl.encode(:y, datum: true_beta)
])
```

## The Tax on Uncertainty

Every dip below the red line triggers the most expensive operation
in the system. Rejuvenation: resample the θ-particles, then run 200
parallel PMCMC proposals. Each proposal evaluates a windowed particle
filter — 100 particles, 20 time steps, 2,000 state propagations per
proposal. Multiply by 200 particles: 400,000 state propagations per
rejuvenation event.

The ESS trace is the cost curve of not knowing the parameters. As the
posterior concentrates, rejuvenation triggers less often. The tax drops.

```elixir
ess_data = result.ess_history
  |> Enum.with_index(1)
  |> Enum.map(fn {ess, t} -> %{"day" => t, "ess" => ess} end)

Vl.new(width: 600, height: 150, title: "Effective Sample Size — below 100 triggers rejuvenation")
|> Vl.data_from_values(ess_data)
|> Vl.layers([
  Vl.new()
  |> Vl.mark(:line, color: "orange", stroke_width: 1.5)
  |> Vl.encode_field(:x, "day", type: :quantitative)
  |> Vl.encode_field(:y, "ess", type: :quantitative, title: "ESS"),
  Vl.new()
  |> Vl.mark(:rule, color: "red", stroke_dash: [4, 4])
  |> Vl.encode(:y, datum: 100)
])
```

## The Accounting

Each of the 80 time steps involved:

**20,000 stochastic simulations** — 200 θ-particles, each propagating
100 state particles through one Binomial SEIR transition. Every
susceptible individual has a probability of exposure computed from
the current θ. Every exposed individual has a probability of becoming
infectious. Integers in, integers out.

**200 likelihood evaluations** — each θ-particle's filter produces an
estimate of p(y_t | y_{1:t-1}, θ), the probability that this day's
case count would arise under this particular guess at the parameters.
The Poisson likelihood bridges the latent I compartment to the noisy
daily report.

**Rejuvenation, when needed** — resample the θ-particles by weight,
then 200 parallel PMCMC moves on all available cores. Each move
proposes a new θ*, runs a windowed particle filter (100 particles ×
20 days = 2,000 propagations), and accepts or rejects by
Metropolis-Hastings. Total per rejuvenation: 400,000 state
propagations, distributed across `System.schedulers_online()` cores.

The window bounds the cost. Day 1 and day 1,000 cost the same.
The posterior at day 79 becomes the prior for day 80. Nothing
is recomputed. Nothing is restarted. The BEAM process that runs
the filter on Tuesday is the same process that ran it on Monday.

## Summary

|                   |                                                                            |
| ----------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| **Algorithm**     | Online SMC² (Chopin et al. 2013, Vieira 2018)                             |
| **Model**         | Stochastic SEIR with Binomial transitions                                  |
| **Observations**  | Poisson-distributed daily case counts                                      |
| **Parameters**    | β (transmission), σ (incubation⁻¹), γ (recovery⁻¹)                |
| **Particles**     | 200 θ × 100 state = 20,000 simulations per step                          |
| **Rejuvenation**  | 400,000 state propagations per event, parallel on all cores                |
| **Why not NUTS?** | Binomial transitions are discrete — no gradient                          |
| **Why BEAM?**     | `Task.async_stream` over 200 particles. If one crashes, the rest continue. |
| **Library**       | smc_ex — pure Elixir, zero dependencies                                  |
